## 6  Step 3 — Create the time range resolver

### When you can extend a base resolver (preferred)

If your rule's time range logic is structurally identical to an existing rule family (daily overtime, weekly overtime, break), extend the base class and override only the abstract `get*RuleType()` method.

**Duration-based example:**
```java
package io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.rule_engine.rules.timerange_resolver.duration_based;

import io.recruitcrm.logging.logger.Logger;
import io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.rule_engine.constants.RuleType;
// Extend the appropriate base for your rule family:
// BaseDailyOvertimeRuleTimeRangeResolver
// BaseWeeklyOvertimeRuleTimeRangeResolver
// BaseBreakRuleTimeRangeResolver

public class DurationBasedMyNewRuleTimeRangeResolver extends BaseDailyOvertimeRuleTimeRangeResolver {

    public DurationBasedMyNewRuleTimeRangeResolver(Logger logger) {
        super(logger);
    }

    @Override
    protected RuleType getDailyOvertimeRuleType() {
        return RuleType.DURATION_BASED_MY_NEW_RULE;
    }

}
```

**Range-based counterpart** — place in `range_based/`, extend `RangeBasedDailyOvertimeRuleRangeResolver`'s base, return `RuleType.RANGE_BASED_MY_NEW_RULE`.

### When you need a custom resolver (fully new logic)

Implement `ICustomRuleTimeRangeResolver` directly and write `resolveTimeRange(TimeRangeResolverContext ctx)`. Use `TimeHelper` and `VirtualTimeRangeAllocator` — do not replicate their logic inline.

```java
package io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.rule_engine.rules.timerange_resolver.duration_based;

import com.google.common.collect.RangeSet;
import com.google.common.collect.TreeRangeSet;
import io.recruitcrm.logging.logger.Logger;
import io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.rule_engine.rules.ICustomRuleTimeRangeResolver;
import io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.rule_engine.rules.timerange_resolver.TimeRangeResolverContext;

import java.time.LocalTime;

public class DurationBasedMyNewRuleTimeRangeResolver implements ICustomRuleTimeRangeResolver {

    private final Logger logger;

    public DurationBasedMyNewRuleTimeRangeResolver(Logger logger) {
        this.logger = logger;
    }

    @Override
    public RangeSet<LocalTime> resolveTimeRange(TimeRangeResolverContext ctx) {
        // Return empty set when this rule does not apply in this context.
        // Return the calculated RangeSet<LocalTime> when it does.
        return TreeRangeSet.create();
    }

}
```

