## 7  Step 4 — Register in `RuleFactory`

File: `rule_engine/RuleFactory.java`

### 4a — Add to the `createRule()` switch

```java
case DURATION_BASED_MY_NEW_RULE:
    return new io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.rule_engine.rules.hourly.MyNewRule(logger);
case RANGE_BASED_MY_NEW_RULE:
    return new io.recruitcrm.microservice.timesheet.rule_engine.rules.shifts.MyNewRule(logger);
```

Place these alongside the other rules of the same family (duration-based block or range-based block).

### 4b — Add to `initializeTimeRangeResolverMap()`

```java
map.put(RuleType.DURATION_BASED_MY_NEW_RULE, new DurationBasedMyNewRuleTimeRangeResolver(logger));
map.put(RuleType.RANGE_BASED_MY_NEW_RULE,    new RangeBasedMyNewRuleTimeRangeResolver(logger));
```

Both rule and resolver must be registered. An unregistered `RuleType` throws `IllegalArgumentException` at runtime when a timesheet with that rule is evaluated.

