## 11  What NOT to do

| Mistake | Why it breaks things |
|---|---|
| Annotating rule or resolver with `@Component` / `@Service` | Rules are created by `RuleFactory`, not Spring — Spring would also create a second uninitialized instance |
| Skipping `initRatesAndMethods()` in `evaluate()` | Rates remain null; `TimeHelper.calculatePayAmount` will NPE at runtime |
| Constructing `RuleEvaluationResult` manually | Use `createCompleteResult()` — it fills `ruleType`, `ruleName`, `virtualRule`, `successful`, metadata |
| Forgetting to register in `RuleFactory` | `RuleFactory.createRule()` hits `default` and throws `IllegalArgumentException` at evaluation time |
| Forgetting `RulePrecedenceConfig` entry | Rule evaluates at `Integer.MAX_VALUE` precedence — it runs last, after `Default Pay`, and claims nothing |
| Placing the new rule after `DEFAULT_PAY` in the precedence list | Default Pay sweeps remaining time first; your rule finds nothing to claim |
| Same ID as an existing `RuleType` | `fromId()` returns the wrong type; pay calculations are silently wrong |
| Testing only `evaluate()` without testing `null` context and `null` custom rule | `BaseRule.validateContext` and `initRatesAndMethods` guard throw `IllegalArgumentException` — missing those paths breaks coverage |
